#{[17592186509527 :question/problem "Let $$\n\\displaystyle a=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle b=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle c=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ Find $(-3)a-((-3)b+3c).$"] [17592186509527 :question/worked-solution "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3)a-((-3)b+3c) & =(-3)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-((-3)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-21 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}-(\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-21 \\\\ -18\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-21 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -18\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-15 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186509551 :question/problem "Let $$\n\\displaystyle a=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle b=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle c=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ Find $5a-(b+5c).$"] [17592186509551 :question/worked-solution "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5a-(b+5c) & =5\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}-(\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}+5\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-15 \\\\ -35\\end{bmatrix}-(\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-30 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-15 \\\\ -35\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-34 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}19 \\\\ -38\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186509711 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186509714 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & {e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y \\\\ {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & {e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186509726 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186509758 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & -{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y \\\\ {e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186509786 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2} & {y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186509789 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & \\operatorname{sin}⁡y \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡y & {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186509813 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-11 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186509818 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 30\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186509845 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 30\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186509863 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & -{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ -{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186509897 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186509943 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186509956 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186509995 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & {e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y \\\\ {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & -{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186510031 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2x & 2y\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186510043 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186510130 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186510135 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}20 \\\\ 30\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186510139 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186510169 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2x \\\\ 2y\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186510189 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2x & 2y \\\\ 2y & 2x\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186510201 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186510203 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186510232 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186510239 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186510364 :question/problem "Given the QR decomposition $A=QR,$ find the least-squares solution to $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nQ=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,R=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186510364 :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is orthogonal, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}x & =\\overbrace{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{{Q}^{T}}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{b} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x & =\\begin{bmatrix}-11 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} &=-11 \\\\ 2{x}_{2} &=10.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=5.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-4(5) & =-11 \\\\ 3{x}_{1} & =9 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $Ax=b$ is $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186510371 :question/problem "$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find  the value of ${q}_{11}\\cdot {r}_{12}.$"] [17592186510371 :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1}\\}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{24}{12}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{3} & 4\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & 4\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{3} & 4\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & 4\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle {q}_{12}\\cdot {r}_{22}=\\left(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)\\cdot (4\\sqrt{3})=-4.\n$$"] [17592186510373 :question/problem "For the transformation $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ {e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\ncalculate ${f}^{′}(x).$"] [17592186510373 :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)=-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & -{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y \\\\ {e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186510378 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u+\\operatorname{cos}⁡w \\\\ v \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡u+w\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186510378 :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u+\\operatorname{cos}⁡w,\\,y(u,v,w)=v,\\,z(u,v,w)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u+w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡w \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0, & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡w \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{2+2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡w \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡w.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186510385 :question/problem "Consider the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={e}^{x+{y}^{2}},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf the tangent to $f$ at $$\n\\displaystyle a=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ is given by\n\n$$\nz-∗=\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}(\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix})\n$$\n\nwhat is the sum of the missing values?"] [17592186510385 :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x+{y}^{2}})={e}^{x+{y}^{2}},\\, & \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x+{y}^{2}})=2y{e}^{x+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{x+{y}^{2}} & 2y{e}^{x+{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f$ and its derivative at $$\n\\displaystyle x=a=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we have\n\n$$\nf(a)=1,\\,{f}^{′}(a)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent to $f$ at $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\nz-f(a)={f}^{′}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f(a),$ ${f}^{′}(a),$ and $a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\nz-1=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}(\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the missing values is\n\n$$\n1+1+2=4.\n$$"] [17592186510396 :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}4x+{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-5y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x+{y}^{2})=4,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x+{y}^{2})=2y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-5y)=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-5y)=-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2y \\\\ 2x & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium $$\n\\displaystyle {x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2(0) \\\\ 2(0) & -5\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, near the equilibrium, the nonlinear system is approximated by\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}u,\n$$\n\nwhere the deviation vector is\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186510419 :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ and \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}xy-{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ -{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy-{y}^{2}-1)=y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy-{y}^{2}-1)=x-2y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+5)=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+5)=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}y & x-2y \\\\ -2x & -2y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium $$\n\\displaystyle {x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}(1) & 2-2(1) \\\\ -2(2) & -2(1)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), so the system is almost linear at ${x}^{∗}.$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ -4 & -2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(-2-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=-2,1.$\n\nFinally, since we have two real eigenvalues with opposite signs, our equilibrium is an $\\text{unstable}$ $\\text{saddle}.$"] [17592186510445 :question/problem "Consider the transformation $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}) \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf the linear approximation of $f$ at $$\n\\displaystyle a=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈\\begin{bmatrix}∗ \\\\ -11\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & 1 \\\\ 2 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}(x-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}),\n$$\n\nwhat is the sum of the missing values?"] [17592186510445 :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2})=\\frac{2}{2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2})=\\frac{1}{2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}(2{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2})=2,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}(2{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2})=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}} & \\frac{1}{2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}} \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f$ and its derivative at $$\n\\displaystyle x=a=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we have\n\n$$\nf(a)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -11\\end{bmatrix},\\,{f}^{′}(a)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe linear approximation of $f$ at $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f(a),$ ${f}^{′}(a),$ and $a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\nf(x)≈\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -11\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}(x-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the missing values is\n\n$$\n0+2+5=7.\n$$"] [17592186510447 :question/problem "Given the QR decomposition $A=QR,$ find the least-squares solution to $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nQ=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{70}}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5} & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3\\sqrt{5} & 3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{5} & -5\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,R=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{70}}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5} & 2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186510447 :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{70}}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5} & 2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}x & =\\overbrace{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{70}}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5} & 3\\sqrt{5} & 2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 3\\sqrt{2} & -5\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{{Q}^{T}}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}^{b} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5} & 2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}x & =\\begin{bmatrix}-10\\sqrt{5} \\\\ -10\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2} &=-10\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{2}{x}_{2} &=-10\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-10.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+2\\sqrt{5}(-10) & =-10\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{5}{x}_{1} & =10\\sqrt{5} \\\\ {x}_{1} & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $Ax=b$ is $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186510452 :question/problem "$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find  the value of ${q}_{22}\\cdot {r}_{22}.$"] [17592186510452 :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1}\\}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{6}{12}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & 0\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle {q}_{22}\\cdot {r}_{22}=\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)\\cdot (2\\sqrt{2})=2.\n$$"] [17592186510461 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u+v\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +w\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 3v \\\\ u\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +w\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nYou may assume that $\\theta $ is a fixed constant."] [17592186510461 :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u+v\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +w\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y(u,v,w)=3v,\\,z(u,v,w)=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =3 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0, & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(-1)^{2+2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186510464 :question/problem "For the transformation $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{3},$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}y-x \\\\ {e}^{x+y} \\\\ {x}^{2}+y\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe total derivative ${f}^{′}$ is given by \n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & \\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}} \\\\ \\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}} & {e}^{x+y} \\\\ 2x & \\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFrom top to bottom, what are the missing entries?"] [17592186510464 :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-x)=-1,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y-x)=1, \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x+y})={e}^{x+y},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x+y})={e}^{x+y}, \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+y)=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+y)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ {e}^{x+y} & {e}^{x+y} \\\\ 2x & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThus, the missing entries are $1,\\,$ ${e}^{x+y},$ and $1.$"] [17592186510466 :question/problem "Consider the transformation $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2}{e}^{{x}_{1}} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3{x}_{2})\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf the linear approximation of $f$ at $$\n\\displaystyle a=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}(x-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}),\n$$\n\nwhat is the sum of the missing values?"] [17592186510466 :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{2}{e}^{{x}_{1}})={x}_{2}{e}^{{x}_{1}},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{2}{e}^{{x}_{1}})={e}^{{x}_{1}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3{x}_{2})=0,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3{x}_{2})=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3{x}_{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2}{e}^{{x}_{1}} & {e}^{{x}_{1}} \\\\ 0 & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3{x}_{2})\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f$ and its derivative at $$\n\\displaystyle x=a=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we have\n\n$$\nf(a)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡6\\end{bmatrix},\\,{f}^{′}(a)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe linear approximation of $f$ at $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f(a),$ ${f}^{′}(a),$ and $a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\nf(x)≈\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡6\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡6\\end{bmatrix}(x-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the missing values is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡6+0+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡6=\\operatorname{sin}⁡6+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡6.\n$$"] [17592186510785 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186510800 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186510837 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6x & -x \\\\ -x & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186510842 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186510861 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186510882 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186510892 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186510902 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186510914 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{13}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 9\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186510921 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186510932 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -x\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186510945 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186510953 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186510973 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186510976 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186510990 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186510992 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186510999 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186511003 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186511006 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & {e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y \\\\ -{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186511027 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}^{2}-y & -x \\\\ -x & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186511038 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6x & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186511039 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186511049 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186511065 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & {e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y \\\\ {e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186511068 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186511095 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}^{2}-y & -1 \\\\ -1 & -x\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186511097 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186511107 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & \\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡y & -{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186511114 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186511121 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186511123 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{13}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186511126 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186511144 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186511147 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & -{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y \\\\ {e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186511163 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186511173 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186511183 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186511202 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186511246 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186511252 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186511264 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{13}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 9\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186511270 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186511276 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & -{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186511289 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186511458 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)=-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & -{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186511466 :question/problem "Find and categorize all the local extrema of the function \n$f(x,y)$ \ngiven that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2}) & -2y{e}^{x}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"]}
